 missing picture is:\n\n![](images/59/59f5625dce754f49724d16b00e839ec34e473601df4702db51f3973f14fd4c10.png)" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192569 1071 "![](images/15/1553e601de2b1664996bb27d49cb09d3baacc72772f9846b45dc45dc9114ff53.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3.$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192569 1071 "![](images/15/1553e601de2b1664996bb27d49cb09d3baacc72772f9846b45dc45dc9114ff53.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3$." 13194139534554 17592186045417 false] [17592186192569 1074 "Since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{6}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9443ceedbda3d541b0fc4d69489eead83dc8550710c7b6b3f1f9ed90d1dc97b.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 15\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{15}{6}\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 false] [17592186192569 1074 "Since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{6}$ by $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9443ceedbda3d541b0fc4d69489eead83dc8550710c7b6b3f1f9ed90d1dc97b.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 15$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{15}{6}\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192570 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b6a481c90b7372d0b78f7029b3fe8ae9c9c98f484459e1b7697308e3e2ebce.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $3$ shaded pieces, and the shape on the right has $6$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}2}$. (That is, the number of shaded pieces has been multiplied by ${\\color{blue}2}$.)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = \\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}2}$." 13194139534554 17592186045417 false] [17592186192570 1074 "We have $\\dfrac{3}{7}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{6}{7}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b6a481c90b7372d0b78f7029b3fe8ae9c9c98f484459e1b7697308e3e2ebce.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $3$ shaded pieces, and the shape on the right has $6$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}2}.$ (That is, the number of shaded pieces has been multiplied by ${\\color{blue}2}.$)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}2}.$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425480 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425481 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425483 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425490 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425491 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192859 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425484 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192859 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425485 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192859 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425488 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192859 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425489 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425479 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425482 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425486 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425487 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186192881 1050 420.0 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193364 1071 "![](images/a0/a013d4e6e0a7aced6662208bb2c1e42c46de68c0f4f8aef360585aa7ea805334.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}$." 13194139534554 17592186045417 false] [17592186193364 1071 "![](images/a0/a013d4e6e0a7aced6662208bb2c1e42c46de68c0f4f8aef360585aa7ea805334.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $2\\times \\frac{2}{5}.$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193364 1074 "Since we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $2$, we repeat the fraction model $2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/edd22792b22b7a3c98b93b1a0baadbfcd79148e6b317ae5b789cb7599a8afb2e.png)\n\n \n\nThere are $2\\times 2=4$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n2\\times \\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193368 1074 "We have $\\frac{3}{8}$ on the left and $\\frac{6}{8}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/67/67b42a3001bad8a95990eb58281c32e1bbde16924ac8d59cddd1082a878fc399.png)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}\\times 2=\\frac{6}{8}.\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193931 1064 "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}\n$$\n\n![](images/74/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\nMultiplying\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](images/88/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces.\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 false] [17592186193931 1064 "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for $\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}$ \n\n![](images/74/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplying $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}$ by $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](images/88/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces. \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}.\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186193932 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 4\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d3c46b3452b6d5d22a30a9949800d7a69bdacf4135615627881c9dc69c0a5513.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $4$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $4$.\n\nOur model has $2$ shaded pieces. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n2 \\times 4 = 8\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](images/b9/b99a75597e9e06cdd0c267b291ae0d671d670f7a3fc4b9af4555d9b217a0b4ed.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\\times 4 = \\dfrac{8}{9}\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 false] [17592186193932 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 4\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{2}{9}.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3c46b3452b6d5d22a30a9949800d7a69bdacf4135615627881c9dc69c0a5513.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $4$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $4.$ \n\nOur model has $2$ shaded pieces. So, the resulting model must have $2 \\times 4 = 8$ shaded pieces:\n\n![](images/b9/b99a75597e9e06cdd0c267b291ae0d671d670f7a3fc4b9af4555d9b217a0b4ed.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\\times 4 = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425408 1080 "3" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425408 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425408 1083 #uuid "04ff24ba-9148-5c9a-a5ed-ceea740a58d9" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425409 1080 "4" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425409 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425409 1083 #uuid "06933fa1-d31f-59f7-9ce2-b976f3f885f9" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425410 1080 "\\frac{5}{8}" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425410 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425410 1083 #uuid "0a8e9f09-c48b-5b4d-a17e-fabfcbcb1a8e" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425411 1080 "\\frac{7}{8}" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425411 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425411 1083 #uuid "0f2c3e19-897f-57d5-af21-ea58951c3506" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425412 1080 "images/74/740fe6c893c603ba078a9d4f5b9a2af4c87236e5740cd3dd1207cac45a6d3aa2.png" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425412 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425412 1083 #uuid "1ecf950a-7dc9-51fb-a22a-decca821ed73" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425413 1080 "\\frac{17}{16}" 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425413 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534554 17592186045417 true] [17592186425413 1083 #uuid "236bf4f1-3ee8-5ea1-a158-13ae378951a3" 13194